-- notes
INSERT INTO concept_section(topic_id, sort_order, title, body_md, status, slug) VALUES ((SELECT id FROM topic WHERE subject_id = (SELECT id FROM subject WHERE slug = 'electric-circuits') AND slug = 'ec1-charge-current-voltage'), 1, 'Charge, current and voltage', '## What is an electric circuit?

Think of the water pipes in your house. A pump pushes water, the water flows through pipes, and somewhere it does useful work (it turns a small wheel, or fills a bucket). An electric circuit is the same idea:

- a **source** (battery, generator) pushes electric charge,
- **wires** carry the charge,
- **elements** (lamp, heater, motor, resistor, capacitor, inductor) use or store the energy that the charge carries.

An electric circuit is a closed path in which charge can flow from the source, through the elements, and back to the source.

In this chapter we study the **building blocks**: the elements and the sources. Every later chapter (KCL, theorems, transients, AC, three-phase) only joins these same blocks in bigger ways. If the blocks are clear, the rest becomes easy.

### Lumped elements: the key assumption

In circuit theory we assume that each element is a **lump**: all its resistance sits in one place, all its capacitance in another place, and the wires between them are perfect (zero resistance, zero length electrically). This is called the **lumped element model**.

It is valid when the circuit is much smaller than the wavelength of the signal. At 50 Hz the wavelength is $\lambda = c/f = 3\times10^8/50 = 6000\ \text{km}$, so a whole substation is "small" and the lumped model works very well. A long transmission line (hundreds of km) is not small, so there we use *distributed* parameters (that is studied in Power Systems).

> **Remember:** All of Electric Circuits (this subject) uses lumped elements. "Distributed" appears only for long lines.

## Charge

Charge is the basic property of matter that causes electric force. Its symbol is $q$ (or $Q$) and its unit is the **coulomb (C)**.

- One electron carries $-1.602\times10^{-19}\ \text{C}$.
- So $1\ \text{C}$ is the charge of about $6.24\times10^{18}$ electrons.

Charge can neither be created nor destroyed. It only moves. This simple fact (conservation of charge) is the real reason behind Kirchhoff''s Current Law, which you will meet at the end of this chapter.

## Current

**Current is the rate of flow of charge.**

$$i = \frac{dq}{dt}$$

Unit: ampere (A). $1\ \text{A} = 1\ \text{C/s}$.

If we know the current, we get the charge that has passed by adding up (integrating):

$$q(t) = \int_{t_0}^{t} i\,dt \;+\; q(t_0)$$

Read this as: **charge = area under the current–time graph**. This one idea solves many exam questions on capacitors, because a capacitor stores exactly this charge.

**Example 1.** A current of 2 mA flows for 30 s. Charge passed $= 2\times10^{-3}\times30 = 0.06\ \text{C}$.

**Example 2.** A current rises in a straight line from 0 to 6 mA in 3 µs. The graph is a triangle, so charge $= \tfrac12\times(3\times10^{-6})\times(6\times10^{-3}) = 9\times10^{-9}\ \text{C} = 9\ \text{nC}$.

### Direction of current

By agreement, the direction of current is the direction in which **positive** charge would move. Electrons actually move the opposite way, but we never need that in circuit work.

When you solve a circuit you may not know the real direction. Don''t worry: **choose any direction** and mark it with an arrow. If your answer comes out negative, the real current flows opposite to your arrow. That is all. A negative answer is not a mistake.

### DC and AC

- **DC (direct current):** constant with time, $i = I$.
- **AC (alternating current):** changes direction periodically, for example $i = I_m\sin\omega t$.
- Many exam questions use other shapes too: ramps, triangles, steps, pulses. The element laws in this chapter work for **any** shape of current or voltage.

## Voltage

To push charge through an element, energy is needed. **Voltage (potential difference) between two points is the energy needed to move a unit positive charge from one point to the other.**

$$v = \frac{dw}{dq}$$

Unit: volt (V). $1\ \text{V} = 1\ \text{J/C}$.

Voltage is always **between two points**. We mark it with a **+** and a **–** sign. $v_{ab}$ means "potential of $a$ minus potential of $b$":

$$v_{ab} = v_a - v_b$$

$$v_{ba} = -v_{ab}$$

So if $v_{ab} = 5\ \text{V}$, then point $a$ is 5 V higher than point $b$.

> **Remember:** Current flows **through** an element. Voltage appears **across** an element (between its two ends). Never mix the two words.

### Ground (reference)

In a big circuit we pick one point and call it **ground** or the **reference node**, with potential 0 V. Every other "node voltage" is measured from this point. Choosing a different ground changes the node voltages, but it never changes any voltage *between* two points, and never changes any current.

## Summary of this section

| Quantity | Symbol | Unit | Key relation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Charge | $q$ | coulomb (C) | $q = \int i\,dt$ (area under $i$–$t$ graph) |
| Current | $i$ | ampere (A) | $i = dq/dt$ (flows through) |
| Voltage | $v$ | volt (V) | $v = dw/dq$ (appears across) |
', 'PUBLISHED', 'main') ON CONFLICT (topic_id, slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, body_md = EXCLUDED.body_md, status = EXCLUDED.status, version = concept_section.version + 1, updated_at = now() WHERE concept_section.body_md IS DISTINCT FROM EXCLUDED.body_md OR concept_section.title IS DISTINCT FROM EXCLUDED.title;
INSERT INTO concept_section(topic_id, sort_order, title, body_md, status, slug) VALUES ((SELECT id FROM topic WHERE subject_id = (SELECT id FROM subject WHERE slug = 'electric-circuits') AND slug = 'ec1-power-energy'), 1, 'Power and energy', '## Power: the rate of using energy

Voltage tells us the energy per coulomb. Current tells us coulombs per second. Multiply them and we get energy per second, which is **power**:

$$p = \frac{dw}{dt} = \frac{dw}{dq}\cdot\frac{dq}{dt} = v\,i$$

Unit: watt (W). $1\ \text{W} = 1\ \text{J/s}$.

Here $p$ is the **instantaneous power**: the power at one instant of time. If $v$ and $i$ change with time, $p$ also changes with time.

## The passive sign convention (the most important rule of this chapter)

An element has two terminals. We mark a voltage $v$ across it (with + and –) and a current $i$ through it (with an arrow). There are only two ways the arrow can sit:

![Passive sign convention: the current arrow enters the terminal marked +](fig:c1-passive-sign)

**Passive sign convention:** the current arrow **enters the + terminal**. When we mark $v$ and $i$ this way:

$$p = v\,i$$

is the power **absorbed** by the element.

- If $p > 0$: the element **absorbs** (takes in) power. A resistor, a charging battery, a capacitor being charged.
- If $p < 0$: the element **delivers** (gives out) power. A battery driving a lamp.

If the current arrow **leaves** the + terminal (enters the – terminal), then $v\,i$ is the power **delivered** by the element, so the absorbed power is $-v\,i$.

> **Remember (two-line rule):**
> Current enters + → absorbed power $= +vi$.
> Current enters – → absorbed power $= -vi$.

Think of it like money. "Absorbed $= -100$ W" means exactly the same as "delivered $= +100$ W", in the same way that "spent ₹ –100" means "earned ₹ 100".

**Example 1.** A 12 V battery has 3 A entering its + terminal (it is being charged). Absorbed power $= 12\times3 = 36$ W. The battery takes in 36 W.

**Example 2.** A 12 V battery has 3 A leaving its + terminal (it is driving a load). Current enters the – terminal, so absorbed power $= -12\times3 = -36$ W, i.e. it **delivers** 36 W.

**Example 3.** An element has $v = 8$ V (+ at top) and a current of 2 A flowing **upward** through it (entering at the bottom, the – terminal). Absorbed power $= -8\times2 = -16$ W. This element is a source: it delivers 16 W.

> **Trap:** Some questions say "power supplied" but write the options as *absorbed* power (a source shown as a negative number), because textbooks such as Sadiku list absorbed power. Work out both sets: absorbed powers, and supplied powers (the same numbers with the opposite sign). If only one set appears among the options, that is the intended one. If both appear, go by the exact words of the question.

## Power balance (conservation of energy)

Energy cannot be created or destroyed. So, at every instant, in any complete circuit:

$$\sum p_{\text{absorbed}} = 0$$

In words: the total power delivered by some elements = the total power absorbed by the others.

This is called **Tellegen''s balance** or simply power balance. It is a powerful check. After solving any circuit, add up the absorbed powers of all elements: the total must be zero.

**Example 4.** A 10 V source drives 2 A through a resistor. Source absorbs $-20$ W (delivers 20 W). Resistor absorbs $+20$ W. Sum $= 0$. ✔

If you are given only **part** of a circuit (a few elements), the sum of their absorbed powers need not be zero, because the rest of the circuit can give or take power.

## Energy

Energy is power added up over time:

$$w = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} p\,dt$$

- For constant power: $w = p\times t$.
- Unit: joule (J). The electricity bill uses kilowatt-hour: $1\ \text{kWh} = 1000\times3600 = 3.6\times10^6\ \text{J}$.

**Example 5.** A constant current of 3 A flows for 20 s through a heater, and 1.8 kJ of heat is produced. Power $= 1800/20 = 90$ W. Voltage across the heater $= p/i = 90/3 = 30$ V.

**Example 6.** A 100 W lamp is used for 5 hours a day for 30 days. Energy $= 100\times5\times30 = 15\,000$ Wh $= 15$ kWh (units).

## Summary of this section

| Idea | Formula / rule |
|---|---|
| Instantaneous power | $p = v\,i$ |
| Passive sign convention | current enters + ⇒ $vi$ = absorbed power |
| Absorbed vs delivered | $p_{\text{abs}} = -p_{\text{del}}$ |
| Power balance | $\sum p_{\text{abs}} = 0$ for a complete circuit |
| Energy | $w = \int p\,dt$; $1\ \text{kWh} = 3.6\ \text{MJ}$ |
