    VALUES (subj, tid, 'NUMERICAL', 'A triangular wave voltage, as shown in figure, is applied across the terminals of a 0.5 F pure capacitor at time t = 0. The corresponding current-wave is', '![Figure](fig:q-ese2018-24)

A triangular wave voltage, as shown in figure, is applied across the terminals of a 0.5 F pure capacitor at time t = 0. The corresponding current-wave is
', '**Idea used:** $i = C\,dv/dt$: the current graph is the slope graph of the voltage graph; a triangle wave gives a square wave (see *The capacitor → Standard waveform pairs*).

Read the voltage: it starts at +10 V at $t = 0$, falls to –10 V at 2 s, rises to +10 V at 4 s, falls to –10 V at 6 s, and so on.

1. **0 to 2 s:** slope $= \dfrac{-10 - 10}{2} = -10$ V/s ⇒ $i = 0.5\times(-10) = -5$ A.
2. **2 to 4 s:** slope $= +10$ V/s ⇒ $i = +5$ A.
3. **4 to 6 s:** $-5$ A; **after 6 s:** $+5$ A.

So the current is a square wave of ±5 A that **starts at –5 A** (because the voltage starts by falling). Options (C) and (D) are triangles (wrong shape). Option (A) starts at +5 A (wrong sign). Option (B) starts at –5 A and switches every 2 s.

**Without a calculator:** Slope $= 20$ V in 2 s $= 10$ V/s, and $0.5 \times 10 = 5$ A. The sign comes from the first part of the wave: falling voltage ⇒ negative current.

**Answer: (B).**
', 'READY', 'MCQ', NULL, NULL, 2, 'UNVERIFIED', 'ese-2018-24', 'PYQ') RETURNING id INTO qid;
  ELSE
