    UPDATE question SET subject_id = subj, topic_id = tid, nature = 'THEORY', summary = COALESCE(NULLIF(summary, ''), 'In the following figure, C₁ and C₂ are ideal capacitors. C₁ has been charged to 12 V before the ideal switch S is closed at t = 0. The current i(t) for all t is'), stem_md = 'In the following figure, C₁ and C₂ are ideal capacitors. C₁ has been charged to 12 V before the ideal switch S is closed at t = 0. The current i(t) for all t is

![Figure](fig:q-gate2012-17)
', solution_md = '**Idea used:** a capacitor voltage cannot jump with finite current; in an ideal circuit with no resistance the jump happens with an **impulse** of current (see *The capacitor → The capacitor voltage cannot jump*).

1. Before $t = 0$: C₁ is at 12 V. C₂''s voltage is not given; it is taken as uncharged, and in any case it is different from 12 V (otherwise nothing would happen).
2. After the switch closes, C₁ and C₂ are in parallel, so by KVL they must have the **same voltage at once**, at $t = 0^+$.
3. So both voltages jump at $t = 0$. A jump of capacitor voltage needs $i = C\,dv/dt$ with $dv/dt$ infinite: an **impulse** of current, carrying a finite charge $q$ in zero time.
4. There is no resistance, so there is no time constant and no exponential decay; after the jump both voltages are equal and constant, so $i = 0$ afterwards.

So $i(t) = q\,\delta(t)$: an impulse. (Option C would be correct only if there were a resistance in the loop.)

**Answer: (D) an impulse function.**
